# Xytro — Completely AI-Managed CPU Scheduler

> Goal: an AI agent **autonomously operates the CPU scheduler end-to-end** — deciding task selection, CPU placement, time slices, and preemption (the actual scheduling), *plus* process lifecycle (kill/start/freeze/priority). It observes, decides, acts, measures, and adapts — with hard safety guarantees and full audit.
> Status: master plan (v2). Architecture: **sched_ext/BPF scheduler in-kernel** + **autonomous steering agent in userspace**. The AI owns the policy; the kernel enforces it safely.

## 1. Vision & scope

- The AI is the scheduler's brain: it sets *how* tasks are chosen and placed, *when* they're preempted, *how long* they run, and *which* processes live/die/freeze.
- Two decision timescales (non-negotiable split):
  - **Micro** (every enqueue/wakeup/tick — millions/sec): a tiny, fast, learned policy runs **in-kernel** via `sched_ext` BPF.
  - **Macro** (100 ms–seconds): the autonomous agent steers the policy, adapts strategy, and manages lifecycle actions from userspace.
- **Fail-safe is built-in**: if the BPF scheduler ever errors, the kernel falls back to the default CFS scheduler — the AI cannot hard-brick the box.
- Out of scope: kernel training, LLM token generation in the per-event path, GPU-accelerated in-kernel inference.

## 2. Architecture overview

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph Kernel
        BPF["xytro_sched (sched_ext BPF)\ntask selection · placement · slices · preemption · balance"]
        MAPS["BPF maps\npolicy weights · strategy · control · stats · telemetry ring"]
    end
    subgraph Userspace
        AGENT["xytro-agent (autonomous brain)"]
        OBS["Observer: workload state, metrics"]
        STEER["Steersman: tune policy / strategy, A/B, rollback"]
        REASON["CoT reasoner + subagents"]
        LIFE["Lifecycle manager: kill / start / freeze / priority"]
        UI["Notification + approval UI"]
        TRAIN["Train pipeline (offline): distill → .bin"]
    end
    BPF --> MAPS
    MAPS --> OBS --> AGENT
    AGENT --> STEER -->|hot-update weights/params| MAPS
    AGENT --> REASON
    AGENT --> LIFE --> UI
    TRAIN -->|new policy weights| STEER
```

The agent steers the scheduler by **writing to BPF maps at runtime** (no recompile, no reload) — that hot-update channel is what makes the system *autonomous*: the AI retunes the live scheduler as the workload changes.

## 3. In-kernel: `xytro_sched` (sched_ext BPF scheduler)

### 3.1 What it controls (real scheduling decisions)
- **Select/dispatch** — which runnable task runs next on a CPU (`ops.select_cpu`, `ops.dispatch`).
- **Placement** — which CPU a task is placed on (`ops.select_cpu`).
- **Time slice** — per-task `slice_ns` (how long before preemption) set at enqueue/dispatch.
- **Wakeup preemption** — whether a woken task preempts the running one (`SCX_ENQ_PREEMPT`).
- **Balance** — cross-CPU load balancing (`ops.balance`).

### 3.2 Policy model (fast, per-event)
- **Learned scoring function**: score(task) = Σ wᵢ·fᵢ in fixed-point int8/int16; dispatch highest score, slice derived from score. Fully tunable and verifier-friendly. The "AI" lives in the weights, which live in a BPF map.
- **Upgrade path**: small int8 MLP (1–2 hidden layers, ~64 units) for scoring, distilled offline from training; forward pass in bounded loops, no floats.
- Weights are read from a BPF map each decision → the userspace agent can **hot-swap the policy live**.
- Hot path never sleeps, allocates, or loops unboundedly.

### 3.3 Telemetry out
- Per-task features (cpu-time delta, state, nice, policy, cgroup weight, ctx-switch rate, rss), decisions taken (who was picked/skipped, slice, preempt), latency samples (wakeup→run), per-CPU util. Delivered via BPF ring buffer + maps to the agent.

### 3.4 Kernel safety properties
- sched_ext **automatic fallback to CFS** on any error.
- BPF **verifier** guarantees bounded loops, safe memory, no unsupported ops.
- No FPU: fixed-point arithmetic only.

## 4. Userspace: `xytro-agent` (the autonomous brain)

### 4.1 Observer
- Consumes telemetry; maintains workload model: phase detection (interactive / batch / RT / power-sensitive), per-task history, interference & latency outliers.

### 4.2 Steersman (macro policy control — the "autonomy")
- Selects **strategy** (e.g. interactive-first, throughput, low-power, RT-aware) by writing a strategy enum + parameters to maps.
- Tunes scoring weights, slice policy, preemption aggressiveness.
- **A/B evaluation**: runs a candidate policy alongside current; compares on the reward metric; **rolls back automatically** on regression.
- Accepts hot model updates from the training pipeline.

### 4.3 CoT reasoner + subagents
- **Macro-cadence SLM (small language model) in userspace** (llama.cpp/GGUF) — *never* in the per-event kernel path (that stays a tiny fixed-point model). Explains decisions, generates `$reason` text, decides strategy switches on ambiguous workload signals.
- **Chosen: Liquid Foundation Model `LFM2.5-230M`** (0.2B, Liquid AI) — their most compact model, distilled from LFM2.5-350M, purpose-built for lightweight on-device agentic pipelines; runs at ~42 tok/s on a Pi 5 (instant on desktop CPU). Official formats: `LFM2.5-230M` (HF, for fine-tuning), **`LFM2.5-230M-GGUF`** (llama.cpp, 24 quantizations). We use the **GGUF quantized to Q3** for minimal memory + CPU at macro cadence (Q4/Q5 if we want more fidelity). Fine-tune via Unsloth/TRL LoRA SFT on the audit-derived reason dataset.
- **Training/quantization pipeline** (`train/reasoner/`): build an action/reason dataset from the audit log + telemetry summaries → fine-tune the LFM (or few-shot) → `llama.cpp` convert + `quantize` to Q3 GGUF → hot-plug into the agent as a `--llm` reasoner (rule-based CoT stays the deterministic fallback).
- Subagent queue (the `<task>$` idea): `reason` (text), `probe` (allowlisted shell inspection), `classify` — each with token/time budgets, reporting back.

### 4.4 Lifecycle manager (the original token protocol)
- `KILL` / `START` / `FREEZE` / `UNFREEZE` / `PRIORITY` actions per §5; kill/start gated by approval; freeze/priority auto with notification.
- Enforced in userspace via signals/`set_user_nice`/`exec`, validated by the safety gate.

### 4.5 Reward / evaluation loop
- `reward = f(interactivity latency, throughput, fairness, power via RAPL)` — the objective the agent optimizes. Metrics drive strategy selection, weight tuning, and rollback.
- **Clarification (what is trained vs rule-based):**
  - *Trained AI today:* the **in-kernel policy** (policy6.bin) — a supervised logistic model trained on labeled traces (80.5% acc).
  - *Rule-based today:* the **reasoner** (deterministic OBSERVE/INFER/DECIDE/REASON) and **lifecycle** hog detection.
  - *The reward is NOT the training signal for policy6.* It is a **hand-defined online scorecard** the agent uses to accept/reject its own live steering deltas (A/B + auto-rollback = a tiny online bandit step). It doubles as the objective for future RL-based policy training/retraining (§7).

## 5. Action protocol (formal schema)

External DSL (reasoner output / logs) stays as the proposed tokens; internal execution uses structured actions. No text parsing in the kernel.

| Plan token | Action (schema) | Runs in | Approval | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `<kill>` | `KILL { pid/name, reason }` | userspace | required | reason generated by reasoner first |
| `<start>` | `START { name/argv }` | userspace | required | delegated launcher |
| `<freeze>` | `FREEZE { pid }` | userspace | auto | SIGSTOP, notify |
| `<unfreeze>` | `UNFREEZE { pid }` | userspace | auto | SIGCONT, notify |
| `<priority>$lower` | `PRIO { pid, -1 }` | userspace | auto | nice step, bounded |
| `<priority>$raise` | `PRIO { pid, +1 }` | userspace | auto | |
| `<priority>$max` | `PRIO { pid, max }` | userspace | required | RT → gated |
| `<priority>$min` | `PRIO { pid, min }` | userspace | auto | nice 19 |
| `<task>$reason&…` | `REASON { action, pid }` → text | userspace | — | CoT subagent |
| `<task>$shell` | `SHELL { allowlisted cmd }` | userspace | required | sandbox + allowlist |

Every action carries `pid`, `reason` (always), `ttl`, and `flags` (auto-approved? / dry-run?).

## 6. Safety & autonomy phases

### Phased autonomy (never unsupervised day one)
1. **Advisory** — AI recommends; nothing executes automatically. Build trust on metrics.
2. **Constrained** — auto within hard bounds: scheduling tuning + freeze/priority only; kill/start still approved; policy deltas clamped.
3. **Full autonomy** — all actions auto within bounds, still audited, still rollback-able, still kill-switch-able.

### Hard guarantees (all phases)
- sched_ext fallback to CFS on error; BPF verifier safety.
- **Protected tasks**: pid 1, kernel threads, the agent's own process, configurable `protect` list — never scheduled out, frozen, or killed.
- **Can't starve the host**: agent runs `SCHED_IDLE` under a cgroup CPU cap; scheduler kthreads have floor priority.
- **Dry-run default**; live requires explicit opt-in.
- **Rate limits** on lifecycle actions; bounded priority deltas per cycle.
- **Audit**: append-only log of every policy change, proposal, approval, and action.
- **TOCTOU**: pids re-validated at action time.
- **Rollback**: last-known-good policy restored automatically on reward regression.

## 7. Training pipeline
- `train/` (offline): collect telemetry traces (M0) → label with a rule-based supervisor or train via RL on the reward metric → **distill** into the tiny fast-policy net → quantize to int8 → export `.bin` weights.
- The agent hot-loads new weights into the BPF map at runtime; no kernel rebuild for model updates.
- Reasoner: pretrained small language model (SLM) — **chosen Liquid Foundation Model `LFM2.5-230M`** — exported as GGUF, quantized **Q3** for minimal footprint (official `LFM2.5-230M-GGUF` exists; Q4/Q5 if more fidelity needed).

## 8. Repository layout (Linux-Xytro)
```
Linux-Xytro/
├── bpf/
│   ├── xytro_sched.bpf.c      # sched_ext scheduler: ops + scoring policy
│   ├── xytro_sched.c          # userspace loader / skel
│   └── maps.h                 # shared BPF maps (policy, strategy, stats, ring)
├── agent/
│   ├── xytro-agent/           # autonomous brain: observer, steersman
│   ├── reasoner/              # CoT model + subagent queue
│   ├── lifecycle/             # kill/start/freeze/priority actions
│   └── ui/                    # notifications + approval
├── train/                     # traces, training, distillation, .bin export
├── proto/                     # shared schemas (BPF maps ↔ agent)
├── tools/                     # load script, systemd units, policy.yaml
└── docs/
```

## 9. CachyOS integration

CachyOS is the ideal target — **no kernel build is required**; its kernels already ship sched-ext compiled in.

- **Use a sched-ext-capable variant:** default `linux-cachyos` (or `-bore` / `-eevdf` / `-lts` / `-server` / `-rc`). **Avoid `linux-cachyos-bmq` and `linux-cachyos-hardened`** — CachyOS documents that these do NOT support sched-ext.
- **The scheduler is a runtime-loaded BPF object, not a kernel patch.** We write it as an `scx` scheduler (BPF core + Rust userspace, following upstream `sched-ext/scx` conventions) and package it in the `scx-scheds` style.
- **Head-start — upstream `scx_forge` already exists:** an AI-oriented sched_ext scheduler + agent (`tools/scx_forge_agent`) that optimizes a workload/goal from a `spec.toml` config. Our plan is more ambitious (full autonomy + CoT reasoner + lifecycle tokens). **Decision at M0/M1:** build on `scx_forge` vs. fork/differentiate.
- **Boot integration:** register with `scx_loader` (`/etc/scx_loader/config.toml`) so it can be set as the default scheduler at boot, and be switchable via `scxctl` / the CachyOS Kernel Manager GUI. Power-profile switching (`power-profiles-daemon`) can map to our strategy modes.
- **Distribution:** ship as a PKGBUILD for the CachyOS repos, or upstream into `scx-scheds-git`.
- **Validation tooling already available:** `cachyos-benchmarker` (full suite) + `schbench` (wakeup latency) for the M5 comparisons; community benchmark site for games.
- If we ever want to change kernel *config* (not needed for v1), sources live at `CachyOS/linux` / `linux-cachyos`, and a custom `CONFIG_CACHY` knob could be added.

## 10. Milestones
- **M6 — Hyprland-style config + last-known-good restore:** scheduler policy is configured with a flat, Hyprland-style `key = value` file (`~/.config/xytro/xytro.conf` — weights, threshold, base_slice_ns, fast_slice_mult, dry_run, optional `policy = *.bin` base; case-insensitive keys, `#` comments). `agent/xytro_config.py` parses/validates/applies atomically via `steer load`, keeps `known_good.bin`+`known_good.conf`+`history.jsonl`+`state.json` beside it, and exposes `show|validate|apply|promote|restore|status|record-exit`. Restore model: `apply` is provisional (snapshots the pre-change live policy as known-good first); the agent promotes on clean A/B; the boot wrapper (`xytro-sched-start.sh`) auto-restores known-good when the previous run exited non-zero (kernel watchdog stall — loader now exits 1 on UEI) or config validation/apply failed, then reverts `xytro.conf` to the known-good text. Config file is the agent's steering baseline. ✅ DONE (verified live: validate/apply/promote/restore round-trip restores the exact pre-change policy; bad values rejected without touching the live policy; `status --broke` tracks stall recovery; agent `THRESHOLD_BASE` reads from config).
- **M0 — Minimal `sched_ext` scheduler + telemetry:** `xytro_sched.bpf.c` that builds, loads, runs (even a pass-through policy), falls back safely; ring-buffer telemetry → `xytro-top` viewer. Proves the mechanism end-to-end. ✅ DONE (verified: builds, loads, CFS fallback on exit, live telemetry).
- **M1 — Learnable policy + steering:** parametric scoring with weights in a BPF map; agent reads/writes them; `xytro-top` + CLI steersman; dry-run + audit + protected list. ✅ DONE (7-feature scored policy, `xytro-steer` hot-update verified live; wakeup-fast-lane rule + **kick-preempt** added → schbench 1ms: p90 7us / p99 161us vs CFS 97us/1606us, RPS 3259 vs 2927 — xytro beats CFS on every wakeup percentile + throughput).
- **M2 — Data + training:** collect traces, train + distill fast policy, quantize, hot-load `.bin` into the map. A/B + rollback harness. ✅ DONE (873MB corpus, `--label tail` avoids wake_freq saturation, `policy6.bin` = general policy acc 80.5%; loaded live; schbench RPS 3062 vs CFS 2927).
- **M3 — Autonomous agent:** observer + reward metrics (latency/throughput/power) + CoT reasoner + strategy selection + auto-steering with guardrails. ✅ DONE (`agent/xytro_agent.py`: observe→phase-detect→reason→steer→A/B→auto-rollback→audit; verified live: applied balanced delta, detected reward 0.631→0.599 regression, auto-rolled-back to policy6).
- **M4 — Lifecycle autonomy:** token protocol, approval UI, subagents, full audit; phased autonomy (advisory → constrained → full). ✅ DONE (`agent/xytro_lifecycle.py`: `list`/`watch`/`freeze`/`unfreeze`/`prio`/`kill`/`start`; token rendering `<freeze>pid=…&reason=…&`; phases advisory/constrained/full; verified live — autonomous watch detected a 101% CPU hog, reasoned, froze it; kill SIGTERM→grace→SIGKILL fixed; prio raise correctly OS-blocked without root; hardened protection: shells/terminals + same-session never touched). **Approval UI + user lists ✅** (`agent/xytro_approval.py`): KILL/START gate on an interactive approval dialog — zenity 4-choice (Approve / Approve & allow always / Deny / Deny & protect always) + a notification-center popup for visibility (COSMIC does not render notification action buttons, hence zenity). Persistent `agent/xytro.xytro` (user lists): `protect` (never touch) + `allow` (auto-approve), with `lock-protect`/`lock-allow` (need `--force`) and a hard-coded CORE list that can never be removed; managed via `protect/allow add|remove|list [--lock] [--force]`, `lists`, or the `tui` menu editor. Verified: CORE/locked removals refused; allow-list short-circuits the dialog (kill → ok, no prompt). Remaining (later): LLM subagents, `start` UX.
- **M5 — Hardening + benchmarks:** stress/fuzz, KASAN/lockdep-style checks on BPF, compare vs CFS (interactivity, throughput, fairness, power), tune reward, systemd boot integration on Xytro. ✅ MOSTLY DONE: **systemd boot integration** (`systemd/install.sh` → `xytro-sched.service` attaches scheduler + hot-loads policy6 + 1ms slice at boot, `xytro-agent.service` runs M3 agent as daemon every 2min with A/B rollback; agent gained `--daemon/--interval`). **Benchmarks vs CFS verified**: wakeup p90 7us/p99 161us/p99.9 687us (CFS 97/1606/1658), schbench RPS 3259 (CFS 2927), stress-ng compute ≈tie with +3.8% efficiency, fairness CV 2.1% (CFS 3.0%). Remaining: power (RAPL) measurement, BPF stress/fuzz (KASAN-style), reward tuning, systemd install validated on a real reboot.

## 11. Risks & mitigations
| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| AI scheduler worse than CFS | Reward-based A/B + automatic rollback to last-good policy (M2/M3) |
| Per-event inference too slow in BPF | Fixed-point linear scoring first; tiny MLP only if it passes latency budget |
| BPF verifier rejects the model | Keep model ≤1–2 layers; fall back to linear scoring |
| Agent bugs destabilize scheduling | sched_ext CFS fallback; BPF verifier; cgroup/SCHED_IDLE caps |
| Destructive lifecycle actions | Approval gates, protected list, dry-run default, TOCTOU recheck, audit |
| Agent starves or kills itself | Protected list includes agent; floor priority; rate limits |
| Floats unavailable in BPF | Fixed-point (int8/int16) quantization from the start |
| Model overfits one workload | Diverse trace corpus, periodic retraining from production telemetry, A/B before promotion |